  <section|Correspondence and mathematical
  organization><label|correspondence-and-mathematical-organization>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><verbatim|eq:ex-cap-rank> is the finite-dimensional rank/readout
    estimate furnished by the selected detector ticket in
    <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.cap>.

    <item><verbatim|eq:ex-cap-tested> is the bounded tested coefficient
    obtained there by subtracting the detector value of the funded difference
    trial and using the owed residual whose budget tends to zero.

    <item><verbatim|eq:ex-cap-remainder> is the scaled remainder estimate. In
    the source it is obtained by setting <verbatim|z=(1+\|\|display(x)\|\|)^{-1}(x-display(x))>.
    The row has bounded coefficient cost because the scalar is at most one
    and the projected row has norm at most one. The sweep estimate applies
    since its projected component vanishes. The detector limit is then made
    at most <verbatim|1/(2Z)>.

    <item>The abstract cap proposition now states the source
    <verbatim|Coin>-type membership for the original row, closure under
    bounded scalar multiplication/subtraction, and inclusion of uniformly
    bounded row families. The paid trial contributes assay values in the
    detector codomain; the separate equation residual compares the detector
    of the source row with those assays. The sweep premise is stated for
    normalized-cost rows with zero projected component. This avoids applying
    a detector defined on coefficient rows to a trial object.

    <item>The proof of <verbatim|lem:ex-fullmesh-cap> uses the exact
    absorption calculation: if <verbatim|X=\|\|display(x)\|\|>, then
    <verbatim|X \<less\>= Zc + Z\|\|L(x-display(x))\|\|> and the scaled
    remainder estimate gives <verbatim|Z\|\|L(x-display(x))\|\| \<less\>=
    (1+X)/2>; rearrangement yields <verbatim|X \<less\>= 2Zc+1>.

    <item>The paragraph after <verbatim|prop:ex-fullmesh-cap-source> records
    the decomposition in <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.improved>: write
    <verbatim|x=display(x)+(x-display(x))>, sweep the kernel term to a
    vanishing owed budget, use the original equation residual as a second
    vanishing owed budget, and close the paid root-test bound under
    addition/subtraction. This is the source's argument turning the finite
    projected-row bound into a paid difference trial.

    <item>The finite-list induction corresponds to
    <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.funding>: its helper list starts with single-slot
    insertion trials; the exact update identity is
    <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.actuate>, and each slot's successive differences
    are paid by <verbatim|YM.PhysicalNext.Repeat.Legato.Run.financed>.

    <item>The two-scale tiling argument corresponds to
    <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.cohort>: choose a fixed tiling span below the
    requested physical separation, then a finer cell span with <verbatim|6n
    a_k\<less\>a_ell>; <verbatim|fits_together> places all points with
    three-cell-span margin; <verbatim|extent> bounds same-cell periodic
    separation by the tiling side <verbatim|T.side> (not the smaller
    recentering margin <verbatim|t>), so strict physical separation makes
    cell assignment injective. Pairwise separation is required for distinct
    slot indices even when insertions coincide;
    <verbatim|YM.Cells.Tiling.planted> gives the product root-test estimate
    under the single common Wilson law. The cutoffs are eventually deep
    enough for the fixed scales for every period, which is the full-mesh
    quantifier in <verbatim|FixedBounds>.

    <item>The final estimate <verbatim|eq:ex-fullmesh-bound> is the
    mathematical content of <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.FixedBounds>. The
    positional construction is downstream:
    <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.positioned> requires both this bound and the
    independently produced same-history Cauchy input
    <verbatim|YM.FullVolume.Purchased>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Assumptions and remaining inputs><label|assumptions-and-remaining-inputs>

  The cap proof uses the actual source's finite selected detector/rank
  ticket, primary paid trial, normalized coefficient-cost stability, and
  uniform same-run sweep estimate. This unit proves the nonlinear absorption
  step and the finite-volume assembly from those receipts; it does not
  reprove their RG production. The public route still depends on the separate
  <verbatim|Purchased> producer for full-mesh Cauchy convergence. No claim is
  made here that <verbatim|FixedBounds> alone implies convergence, or that
  the finite volume threshold is uniform over unbounded field/test families.

